Subject: [PATCH 0/2] soundwire/SDCA: enable the four TAS2783 speakers on HP OmniBook Ultra 14

The HP OmniBook Ultra 14 (DMI board 8EB4, BIOS F.06, Intel Panther Lake,
Core Ultra X9 388H, sof-audio-pci-intel-ptl) has four TI TAS2783 SmartAmp
peripherals, two on SoundWire link 1 and two on link 2, plus an RT712 on
link 3. On a stock kernel there is no speaker output: no SmartAmp SDCA
function is created, and even when one is, three of the four amplifiers
drop UNATTACHED ~100-250 ms after attaching and firmware download fails.
Reported at https://github.com/thesofproject/linux/issues/5732 (also
#5877 and #5802).

Two independent problems, one patch each:

1. The BIOS declares the SDCA Function Topology control (0x05) for the
   TAS2783 nodes with access mode 0x03 and no DC value, so snd-soc-sdca
   refuses to create the function. This is the same defect as
   thesofproject/linux#5760, where the outcome was a BIOS fix for the HP
   EliteBook X; the newest 8EB4 BIOS (F.06, 2026-06-25) still lacks the
   DC value. Patch 1 adds a minimal known-peripheral fallback. I am fine
   dropping it if HP commits to a BIOS update for 8EB4 - sending it so
   the machines in the field have something in the meantime.

2. The static DSDT declares intel-sdw-doais = 1 / intel-sdw-doaise2 = 1
   for every link, but SNDW._INI overwrites the LNKn packages from NVS
   and the driver reads doais = 3 / doaise2 = 0. The amplifiers cannot
   follow that ACTMCTL drive timing. Bisecting the fields at runtime:
   DOAIS = 1 together with DOAISE2 = 1 is both necessary and sufficient;
   DODS, CLDS and CLDE are irrelevant; DOAISE2 gates whether DOAIS is
   honoured. Patch 2 adds a DMI-keyed override for the two fields and
   applies it to links 1 and 2 of this board.

Tested on v7.2.2 with otherwise stock soundwire-bus and
snd-soc-tas2783-sdw modules and only these two changes: all four
amplifiers attach and stay attached, firmware loads on the first
attempt, zero PARITY errors at boot and after streaming, and each of
the four speakers plays when driven alone. The bus enumerates at the
ACPI-default 9.6 MHz; no clock change is needed.

I am not sure a DMI quirk table in intel_auxdevice.c is the shape you
want for this; happy to rework it (dmi-quirks.c, a machine-driver quirk,
or a generic "trust the DSDT over NVS" option) if you prefer.

ACPI dumps, the Windows-side comparison and UCM files:
https://github.com/kaseyprichard/hp-omnibook-tas2783-sdw


Kasey Prichard (2):
